@@ -0,0 +1,301 @@
+# ETOE-005 — Disposable dogfood fixture and deterministic baseline
+
+> **Scope.** This report records a reproducible, disposable SOP dogfood fixture
+> created **outside any production checkout**, containing one success task, one
+> intentionally failing task, and one human-gated task, with their expected
+> outcomes declared **before execution**. It records the exact setup/run/teardown
+> commands, the fixture root, the binary provenance, the run-id capture surface,
+> and the isolation/mutation boundaries.
+>
+> **Isolation boundary.** The governed implementation harness is repository-root
+> confined (`internal/toolharness/files.go`, `internal/toolharness/harness.go`
+> reject `../` escapes and unauthorized roots). Fixture creation and execution
+> therefore cannot be performed by the governed agent inside the agentic-sop
+> checkout. The fixture is created and run by the operator-run scripts in
+> `scripts/` against a **configurable disposable root** (default
+> `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/etoe-005-fixture`). No fixture mutation is an in-repo side
+> effect, and the scripts refuse any fixture root that resolves inside the
+> production checkout.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Binary provenance
+
+The fixture can be driven by either the installed `sop` binary or a source build
+from HEAD. When a required surface is missing from the installed binary
+(`run --max-tasks`, `approval supersede`), the source build is used.
+
+| Item              | Value                                                                       |
+| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| Installed binary  | `sop` on `PATH` (e.g. `/Users/imhttran/go/bin/sop`), `sop version` = `sop dev` |
+| Source build      | `go build -o /tmp/sop-etoe005 ./cmd/sop`                                    |
+| Source binary ver | `sop version` = `sop dev`                                                   |
+| Go toolchain      | required to build the source binary (available; go1.27.1 observed)          |
+
+Build command (verbatim):
+
+```bash
+go build -o /tmp/sop-etoe005 ./cmd/sop
+```
+
+The setup and run scripts accept the binary as their second positional argument
+or via `ETOE005_SOP_BINARY`, so an operator chooses the installed binary or the
+source build explicitly. This report records the binary provenance and the
+capture procedure; the concrete binary used for a given capture is recorded in
+the run-records file (`<fixture-root>/etoe-005-run-records.txt`).
+
+---
+
+## 2. Fixture root and configuration
+
+| Setting            | Default                              | Override                        |
+| ------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------- |
+| Fixture root       | `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/etoe-005-fixture`   | positional arg 1 / `ETOE005_FIXTURE_ROOT` |
+| `sop` binary       | `sop` on `PATH`                      | positional arg 2 / `ETOE005_SOP_BINARY`   |
+| Force recreate     | off (reject existing root)           | `ETOE005_FORCE_RECREATE=1`      |
+| Run records file   | `<fixture-root>/etoe-005-run-records.txt` | `ETOE005_RECORDS`          |
+
+The fixture root is resolved to an absolute path and is the **only** location the
+setup/run/teardown scripts mutate outside their own repository checkout. All
+three scripts refuse a fixture root that resolves inside the production checkout.
+The in-checkout guard compares the resolved fixture root against the resolved
+checkout root (the parent of the `scripts/` directory), so a fixture root
+anywhere inside the checkout is rejected.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Setup, run, and teardown commands (verbatim)
+
+### 3.1 Setup
+
+```bash
+# Optional: build the source binary (only if a required surface is missing from
+# the installed binary).
+go build -o /tmp/sop-etoe005 ./cmd/sop
+
+# Create the disposable fixture (default root is a temp location).
+scripts/etoe-005-fixture-setup.sh /tmp/etoe-005-fixture /tmp/sop-etoe005
+```
+
+Setup:
+
+1. rejects an existing fixture root unless `ETOE005_FORCE_RECREATE=1`;
+2. creates the fixture root and runs `sop init` there, producing
+   `.agent-sdlc/state.db` and `.agent-sdlc/config.yaml` (re-running `init` is
+   safe);
+3. writes the minimal Go module (`go.mod`, `calc.go`, `calc_test.go`) plus a
+   disabled always-failing test (`broken_test.go.disabled`);
+4. writes the three task files under `tasks/` and the fixture PLAN under
+   `docs/PLAN.md`;
+5. **intentionally replaces** `.agent-sdlc/config.yaml` with the fixture's own
+   complete, deterministic configuration (a full, deliberate write — the fixture
+   must pin deterministic validation commands). The written shape follows the
+   documented schema and is validated immediately by `sop status`, which catches
+   schema drift or unknown keys at setup time. The config sets deterministic
+   local `validation` (`go build ./...`, `go test ./...`, `go vet ./...`),
+   `human.approval_before_commit: true`, and `workflow.mode: local`.
+
+### 3.2 Expected `sop init` layout
+
+```text
+.agent-sdlc/
+  state.db
+  config.yaml
+```
+
+### 3.3 Run (executes the three tasks and captures run IDs)
+
+```bash
+scripts/etoe-005-fixture-run.sh /tmp/etoe-005-fixture /tmp/sop-etoe005
+```
+
+The run script:
+
+1. performs the FIX-FAIL enabling step deterministically (renames
+   `broken_test.go.disabled` → `broken_test.go`) before running FIX-FAIL, and
+   keeps the failing test disabled for FIX-SUCCESS;
+2. runs `sop run --task tasks/FIX-SUCCESS.md`, then `tasks/FIX-FAIL.md`, then
+   `tasks/FIX-GATE.md` against the fixture project;
+3. records, for each task, the run ID (newest directory under
+   `.agent-sdlc/runs/`) and the observed outcome read from the run's
+   `state.json`/`sop report`/`sop approvals --json`, appending to
+   `<fixture-root>/etoe-005-run-records.txt`;
+4. never invents a value: anything that cannot be read is recorded as
+   `UNAVAILABLE`.
+
+### 3.4 Teardown
+
+```bash
+scripts/etoe-005-fixture-teardown.sh /tmp/etoe-005-fixture
+```
+
+Teardown removes **only** the fixture root. It refuses an unsafe path
+(`/`, `/tmp`, `/var`, `/usr`, `/etc`, `/Users`) or a path inside the production
+checkout, is idempotent (missing root ⇒ exit 0), and reports whether removal
+succeeded.
