+## 4. Fixture tasks and predeclared expected outcomes
+
+Exactly three tasks. Outcomes were declared in the fixture files **before** any
+execution (see `tasks/FIXTURE.md`, `tasks/FIX-SUCCESS.md`, `tasks/FIX-FAIL.md`,
+`tasks/FIX-GATE.md`).
+
+| Task          | Kind             | Declaration of intent                                                              | Expected outcome (predeclared)                       |
+| ------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
+| `FIX-SUCCESS` | success          | Implement `Add`; keep `go test ./...` passing.                                     | `completed` / `LOCAL_DONE`; validation **PASS**      |
+| `FIX-FAIL`    | intentional fail | Enable `broken_test.go.disabled` → an always-failing test.                          | validation **FAIL** / run outcome `BLOCKED`          |
+| `FIX-GATE`    | human-gated      | Reach the human approval boundary (`human.approval_before_commit: true`).          | `needs_human` gate: approval request requiring a human |
+
+The intentionally failing task fails through SOP's **deterministic** validation /
+quality gate (`go test` fails deterministically), not through model interpretation.
+The enabling rename is performed by the run script, so it is scripted rather than
+left to ad-hoc manual execution.
+
+### 4.1 Why the failing task is deterministic
+
+The failing check is `go test ./...` from `.agent-sdlc/config.yaml`:
+
+```yaml
+validation:
+  build:
+    - go build ./...
+  test:
+    - go test ./...
+  lint:
+    - go vet ./...
+```
+
+`TestBroken` calls `t.Fatalf` unconditionally, so the test command exits non-zero
+regardless of the model, and the quality gate classifies the task FAIL deterministically.
+
